{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n66 \n \n \nbottle at CIR Manifold #4 to prepare for calibration test points for the next ACME experiment, Flame \nDesign Part 2. \n \nWeek 3: Oct. 29 \u2013 Nov. 4 \nOn GMT 301, we began the second round of testing for ACME Flame Design with 18 normal flames ignited \nusing propane fuel. The goal of Flame Design is to improve our understanding of soot processes so that \nflames can be \u201cdesigned\u201d to be soot free. The remaining tests will look at inverse flames, which are formed \nwhen diluted oxygen flows from the burner into a Combustion Chamber filled with diluted fuel. Compared \nto normal flames, inverse flames are inside out so soot is formed outside rather than inside the flame. \nWhile soot processes are known to depend on flame temperature and the residence time of the fuel at \nhigh temperatures, a comparison of normal- and inverse-flame results will help us better understand the \nimpact of flow direction on soot processes. On GMT 306 and 307, Shane Kimbrough, Megan McArthur \nand Mark Vande Hei configured the ACME Chamber Insert and replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold \n#2 and CIR Manifold #4 to support the inverse flame tests for Flame Design Part 2. \n \n \nFigure 1. Flame Design Normal Propane Flame \n \nThis false-colored image of a soot-free spherical flame shows an array of 14-micron ceramic fibers making \ntemperature measurements. The blue in the image has been enhanced so that the dim flame is visible in \naddition to the fibers. Normally, the hot fibers appear to be red, orange, and yellow\u2014in order of increasing \ntemperature\u2014just like metal in a forge. The temperature profile along each fiber is optically determined \nfrom its varying hue through a technique known as Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP). \n \nThe base of the flame has locally quenched due to heat loss to the burner\u2019s supply tube, which created a \nhole in the flame. This hole allowed for direct mixing of the fuel and oxygen, which enhanced flammability \nand causes the flame to momentarily reform. In this test, the spherical flame repeatedly opened and \nclosed with oscillations that grew in strength until the entire flame extinguished.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 66}}